      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Да, с большим удовольствием.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я уверен, тебе очень понравится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Твоя еда всегда самая лучшая.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Кристина, ты знаешь одну вещь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Kristina, do you know one thing?' Here 'Kristina' sets up the idea, while 'thing' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Какую именно вещь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Which exact thing?' You'll hear 'thing' all the time in the show, here connected with 'thing'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я действительно неплохо готовлю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I truly cook not badly.' In Russian, 'I' leads into 'prepare' in a very natural everyday phrasing. Kristina answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Да, я это прекрасно знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, I know that perfectly well.' Kristina starts with 'I' and pairs it with 'this' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это очень вкусные роллы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'These are very tasty rolls.' The key word here means 'this', which Chef Viktor combines with 'very much'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Ты приготовил их сам?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did you prepare them yourself?' The key word here means 'you', which Kristina combines with 'cooked'. Then Chef Viktor replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, специально для тебя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, specially for you.' Chef Viktor puts 'on purpose' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Они выглядят очень аппетитно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They look very appetizing.' You'll hear 'they' all the time in the show, here connected with 'appetizingly'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хочешь немного попробовать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you want to taste a little?' The first content word means 'do you want', and 'to taste' rounds out the thought. And Kristina jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Да, с большим удовольствием.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, with great pleasure.' A compact expression built around 'great' and 'pleasure'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я уверен, тебе очень понравится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am sure you will like it very much.' The first content word means 'I', and 'will please' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Твоя еда всегда самая лучшая.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Your food is always the best.' A compact expression built around 'your' and 'best'. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
